WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.610
CYNTHIA THOMAS: Welcome to
NET 101, hybrid networking

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:11.580
for millions of users with
GCP and Twitter engineering.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.030
My name is Cynthia Thomas,
I'm a networking specialist

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:15.800
at the Google Cloud.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.595
And I'm accompanied by Todd.

00:00:17.595 --> 00:00:18.970
TODD SIEGEL: Hi,
I'm Todd Siegel.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.470
I'm a software engineer at
Twitter working on traffic

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.885
and our edge infrastructure.

00:00:23.885 --> 00:00:25.260
CYNTHIA THOMAS:
Today we're going

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.870
to talk to you about how
Twitter manages traffic

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.440
to handle millions of tweets,
and leverages Google networking

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.260
to augment that capacity.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.010
Let's get into it.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.050
Twitter is leveraging
Google Cloud products

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.470
that are built on Google's
proven infrastructure.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.750
You're probably able to
recognize some of these logos

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:45.590
here.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:49.130
You supported over a billion
users on nine products,

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.720
all relying on Google
networking infrastructure.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.910
We've learned a lot
and innovated a lot

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.100
to be able to
achieve such scale,

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.760
while ensuring reliability,
speed, and security.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.040
That means better
reliability and performance

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.560
than the public internet
and other cloud providers.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:11.790
Through this
experience, we've been

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.490
able to develop protocols
that improve networking

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:18.030
performance like BBR and QUIC.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.400
BBR improves the previous
TCP congestion algorithm,

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.820
which was based on packet loss.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.130
Instead, Google has
designed an algorithm

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:30.150
to respond to actual congestion
for available bandwidth

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:35.230
and minimal roundtrip times
for huge performance gains.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.920
Another example of Google
networking innovation is QUIC.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:44.140
Based on UDP, a transport layer
protocol that reduces latency

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.330
by eliminating the
number of handshakes

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:50.070
compared to HTTPS over TCP.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.930
Twitter and Google
Cloud users already

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.120
benefit from these
higher throughput

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.450
lower latency gains by
accessing Google managed

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.855
services like Bigtable
and Cloud storage.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.560
By the current numbers
we have 24 Cloud regions

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.620
across the globe and several
more are coming down the pike.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.720
As you can see, Google's
made large investment

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.200
in infrastructure globally,
including undersea cables,

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.630
Cloud regions,
partnerships of ISPs

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:23.720
is to deploy, POPs
and CDNs of the edge.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.970
We continue to grow our
data center and Cloud region

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.740
count each year.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.200
This benefit means we can
rely on Google engineering

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.850
to deploy lively and
securely across the globe.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.650
It's benefit, for
example, a Twitter user

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.410
anywhere in the world for
a better user experience.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.570
Here we can see Google's
made a sizable investment

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.250
in physical
infrastructure globally.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.700
Google has built
its own backbone

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:56.240
providing private connectivity
between Google Cloud regions,

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.810
data centers, and such.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.500
Just as Twitter has leverage
Google infrastructure

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.830
for cloud services like Cloud
Bigtable, edge networking,

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.470
Cloud storage,
Google Cloud users

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.860
can also benefit from Google's
dedicated infrastructure

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:15.060
for performance and security.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.310
As Todd will discuss,
Twitter has complemented

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.980
its own infrastructure
by bursting and adding

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.820
capacity using Google's
global footprints.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.980
Twitter is already taking
advantage of Google edge

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.430
networking including our
POPs, for private dedicated

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:34.420
interconnects peering at or
Google finance for caching,

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:37.495
load balancing, and securing
against DDOS at the edge.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.600
One major differentiator
for Google Cloud networking

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.607
is the Shared Global VPC model.

00:03:47.607 --> 00:03:49.940
So Twitter takes advantage
of this model where the share

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:53.810
VPC allows centralized
management of Google Cloud

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.490
networking.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:57.640
So that means subnets
and routes are

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.610
reachable across the
entire global of VPC, which

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.740
means great ease of
management while never

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.820
traversing the public internet.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.740
Additionally this
means you can deploy

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:13.840
Google global constructs
like the Google Global Load

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.810
Balancers to deploy
at any cost VIPs,

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.640
minimizing the amount
of configuration,

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.029
and providing a great
end user experience.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:28.720
So there is a host of Google
Cloud networking products

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.050
and capabilities that Google
Cloud provides powered

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:34.730
by our proven infrastructure.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.680
We have various ways
to connect your on-prem

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:41.570
to our cloud, security workloads
with numerous security options,

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.540
and scale applications through
our load balancers and Cloud

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:45.860
CDN.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:49.160
And furthermore take advantage
of orchestrate and workloads

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.520
through open source
standard platforms

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.280
like Kubernetes and
Istio, the roots

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.700
of which come from Google.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.360
So during this
talk, we'll discuss

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.460
some of the edge networking
features that Twitter

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.610
leverages from Google to scale.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:09.800
This includes the Google global
load balancer, and Cloud CDN.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.220
Next, Todd will get into how
Twitter deploys at the edge.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:17.090
TODD SIEGEL: Thanks, Cynthia.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:20.690
So I'm going to talk a little
bit about Twitter's edge

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.560
network before we expanded
our network with Google,

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:26.570
and some of the issues
that we were facing

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:28.220
and how we solved them.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.310
So Twitter's global
edge network is

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:33.560
made of multiple
points of presence

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.510
deployed throughout the world.

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:39.200
And we also have a
global backbone network

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.510
that this edge connects to.

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:44.660
The edge then forums traffic
on to our serving regions

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:46.670
that are located in a
few different places

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:49.160
throughout the world.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.070
When we originally
deployed this network

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:55.730
we had different IP addresses
in different locations, Unicast

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.520
IPs or regional IPs,
and then we would

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:02.150
have different DNS responses
based on what the user was.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.040
We eventually change
this to be globally

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.160
any cast VIPs, which greatly
improved availability

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:10.160
because of some of the issues
with DNS caching or imprecise

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:11.750
geolocation.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:14.190
So availability was
good at this point,

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:16.460
however performance
wasn't always good.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:20.090
So some of the challenges
that we've run into

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:23.840
are first of all Anycast is
optimized by the carriers

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.210
by the individual
service providers.

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:30.860
They can set their own policies
Twitter can't necessarily

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:34.040
control making sure
that a certain service

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.410
provider provides the best
experience for Twitter

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.870
users versus other users.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:42.080
The service provider may
optimize for their own traffic

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.480
or for different types of
traffic versus Twitter traffic.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:48.920
Another issue is with
capacity and with location,

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:50.960
getting close to the users.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.130
If Twitter wants to add a
new location on our network,

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.980
we have to do a lot
of research and we

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:02.490
have to incur a lot of
cost in engineering.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.880
So it's difficult
if the World Cup

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:07.040
is going to be in a
new location where

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:10.490
we don't have good
connectivity or a good POP--

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:12.230
or sorry, a local POP.

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:15.113
Or the Olympics are going
to be somewhere again, where

00:07:15.113 --> 00:07:17.030
it's challenging for
Twitter users to connect.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:21.260
It's difficult for us to quickly
add a new location, especially

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.840
if it's going to be somewhere
temporary where we just

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.150
need a burst of capacity.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:29.530
Let's look a little
bit into some

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:32.770
of the problems with
Anycast IPs that we've seen.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:36.020
So with our Anycast
IPs, our end users--

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:40.270
so here, let's assume
that this purple icon

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:42.520
is a user somewhere
in Europe that's

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.380
trying to connect
with the Twitter edge.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.990
They have the Twitter
global Anycast IP,

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:50.470
and that edge can resolve to
one of n different regions.

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:54.670
One of n different edges
that exist in Europe.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.310
It's up to this blob
called the internet

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.935
to figure out if this will
go to Edge A or Edge B

00:07:59.935 --> 00:08:01.815
and the path it
takes to get there.

00:08:01.815 --> 00:08:03.190
And this blob is
actually made up

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:06.160
of lots of different hops,
individual service providers,

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:09.880
transit providers, and various
agreements between them

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:11.313
on how traffic is rabid.

00:08:11.313 --> 00:08:13.230
This can change throughout
the course of a day

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:17.350
and based on a variety
of settings that

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:19.820
are made by people frequently.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:21.670
So the path from the
end user to Twitter

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:26.110
is not always going to
be the most optimal path.

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:28.540
Additionally, when Twitter
has to send the response back

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:33.400
to the end user, Twitter can
just choose the first hop back.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:37.809
Then that one may take
a circuitous route back

00:08:37.809 --> 00:08:39.549
to the end user.

00:08:39.549 --> 00:08:41.380
Again, both of these
directions may not

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.950
be optimal at a
given point in time.

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:47.320
They could be based on time of
day, network feedback, round

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.700
robin algorithms, a
variety of things.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:52.060
So this is pretty
good for availability,

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:54.640
but it's not great for latency.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.390
Furthermore, it doesn't always
seem logical to our engineers,

00:08:58.390 --> 00:08:59.980
or at least to me.

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.220
This chart shows where
requests from users

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:07.090
in South Korea, which
Twitter edge the request

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:09.670
ended up on in the
course of a day,

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:11.590
during the course of a day.

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:14.440
I personally would have expected
almost all of the requests

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:19.330
to show up at our edge location,
which is in eastern Asia.

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:21.070
We have multiple
locations in Asia,

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:24.130
I would have expected
that the South Korean user

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:25.450
requests would end up there.

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:28.780
Turned out only
about 60% of requests

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:31.990
ended up there, where
the other 40% of requests

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:35.530
ended up going to Europe
or going to the US.

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:39.310
We also noticed a
lot of deviation

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:41.620
in the overall
latency histograms,

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:43.150
large standard deviation.

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:46.180
Some of this is attributed to
the return path that Twitter

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:49.810
was taking, or just some
of these really long paths

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:51.850
that the user
requests were taking.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:55.060
So improvements that
we've made to mitigate

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.750
some of these problems.

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:58.990
One of them has been
to expand our edge

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:02.410
by using the public cloud,
specifically the Google Cloud,

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.340
the Google load balancers.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:07.790
So this is really increased
our global footprint.

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:11.350
So however many POPs we
had before, now we also

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:14.830
have the entire Google POP
network as well to ingress

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:16.420
user traffic.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.970
With all of Google's
BGP optimizations

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:23.230
and peering agreements with
the various service providers,

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:26.240
and the return paths as well.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:31.150
Also we've acknowledged that
we can't control making sure

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:34.000
that service providers
provide the optimal path

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:36.680
from a specific user to Twitter.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:38.290
So what we've decided
to do is we've

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:41.200
setup a variety of
different VIPs, instead

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:43.720
of a single global Anycast
VIP, or a single set

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.180
of global Anycast VIPs.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:48.490
And what we've
done is we measure

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:50.440
all of these different
VIPs, and then we

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:53.140
can optimize how an
individual user connects

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:56.600
to Twitter by applying the
best VIP for that user.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:11:00.173
So looking back at this
diagram again, the end user,

00:11:00.173 --> 00:11:01.840
we can give them our
global Anycast VIP.

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:03.382
They can connect
through the internet

00:11:03.382 --> 00:11:06.373
through all of those hops and
end up at Edge A or Edge B,

00:11:06.373 --> 00:11:07.790
and then we can
return it to them.

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:11.770
We can also provide
another in this case,

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:14.320
this VIP also took the
same path to Edge A,

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:18.820
but perhaps in this case
Twitter's BGP policy says

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:22.800
that the return path will always
go to a specific provider.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:26.650
And we can measure how this
red and this black path

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:28.400
are performing over
the course of the day.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:29.817
And we can prefer
one or the other

00:11:29.817 --> 00:11:31.730
for our individual end users.

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:33.910
We can also set
up Anycast VIPs so

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:36.490
that our user is
making a request

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:39.700
and it forces them to land
on Edge B in this case,

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:41.200
or perhaps it doesn't work.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:44.020
We can, again, measure it
and we can provide this

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:46.330
if it's a good option
for the end user.

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:48.280
Also, we can set up
lots and lots of VIPs

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:50.890
in the public Cloud that
are up to Google edges,

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:52.990
and then also provide
Google various ways

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:56.720
to route back to Twitter and
measure all these as well.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.020
The system we've set
up that uses real time

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:04.210
measurements to choose from one
of hundreds of different VIPs

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:06.890
or routes back to Twitter
for individual end users,

00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:09.100
we call it the
traffic control tower.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:14.230
We steer individual users
to the best VIP for them

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.150
at that moment.

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:18.490
We use a variety of information.

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:24.040
We use net blocks, something
that's ISP identifiers,

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:27.160
and country codes to figure
out how to group measurements

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:28.330
the other.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:29.953
We're able to route
around problems

00:12:29.953 --> 00:12:31.120
that we see in the internet.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.100
We're able to find optimized
routes from a user to Twitter

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:39.100
and back, and it's proven
very effective for us.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:41.140
I'm going to hand it
back over to Cynthia

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:42.820
to talk a bit about
the Google network.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:44.920
And then we'll look at
how Twitter specifically

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:45.920
uses the Google edge.

00:12:48.523 --> 00:12:49.690
CYNTHIA THOMAS: Thanks, Tom.

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:51.370
So yeah, as Tom
mentioned, the internet

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:54.260
can definitely present itself
with inconsistent behavior

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:55.990
on the traffic path.

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:57.670
So clearly the
network matters and is

00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:00.220
really important to the
end user experience.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:01.990
With Google's
private backbone, we

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:05.290
can provide two types
of networking tiers.

00:13:05.290 --> 00:13:08.030
So there's the standard
and premium tier.

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:10.000
So the Premium tier is
a unique combination

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:12.820
of providing speed
and reliability,

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:15.740
riding the Google
private backbone.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:18.490
So the best analogy is pretty
much like a hot potato,

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:20.510
cold potato type behavior.

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:22.330
So the default
alternate behavior

00:13:22.330 --> 00:13:25.150
would be like cold
or hot potato,

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:28.180
getting rid of the packets and
handing off to the internet

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:30.020
as soon as possible.

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:32.740
Whereas with the
Google premium tier,

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:35.590
we basically hang onto the
packet as long as possible,

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:38.290
deliver it, ride it over
our private backbone,

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:41.380
and then deliver it as close
to the destination or user

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:42.830
as possible.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:46.030
So this enables basically
a better reliability,

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:49.570
performance, security, and
thus a better user experience.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:54.680
So one networking contract
that Google provides

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:58.380
is the global load
balancer with Anycast it.

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:01.730
So this load balancer can
use to for Global reach,

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:05.260
for users to quickly
enter the Google backbone

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:07.970
and infrastructure in
traverse the private network

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:10.620
to its destination.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:14.520
It provides worldwide capacity,
cross region failover,

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:18.440
and fast auto scaling, and the
ability to provide or handle

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:20.450
millions of queries per second.

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:24.020
So Twitter actually leverages
the Google Cloud load balancer

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:27.740
for edge reach
ability of services.

00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:32.030
And basically this
Global load balancer

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:35.390
can be combined with both
Cloud CDM and Cloud Armor

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:38.510
for both caching and security
services while scaling.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:45.900
So one powerful behavior
of the global load balancer

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:50.590
is the cross region failover,
the overflow in other regions.

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:52.650
So traffic is actually
a proxy to back ends

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:57.310
by a special algorithm called
the waterfall by region.

00:14:57.310 --> 00:15:00.450
So if an entire region
reaches capacity,

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:03.060
the waterfall by region
algorithm is triggered

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:04.980
and traffic can
overflow to the closest

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:08.210
region with available capacity.

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:10.760
And traffic can also be
preemptively overflowing

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:16.100
to other regions in the event
of unhealthy back ends in this

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:18.050
I just want to talk a
little bit about NEGs,

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:19.580
so Network Endpoint Groups.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:21.230
This is introduced
actually helps

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:24.350
a lot in containerized
environments

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:28.310
where without NEGs, there is
an IP tables rule that would

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:30.150
hop from one node to another.

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.240
So there is this
extra hop introduced.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:33.830
Network Endpoint
Groups is basically

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:38.030
a collection of endpoints
defined as an IP port pair.

00:15:38.030 --> 00:15:39.470
This allows things
like pods to be

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:44.492
directly reachable as back ends
to the global load balancer.

00:15:44.492 --> 00:15:45.950
So what's interesting
about that is

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:48.200
you can use NEGs,
and other definitions

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:51.020
as well like
internet NEGs, which

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:53.580
would align to endpoints
that are actually outside

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.845
[INAUDIBLE].

00:15:55.845 --> 00:15:57.470
So next time we'll
get into the details

00:15:57.470 --> 00:16:00.510
of how Twitter uses Google
Cloud load balancing

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:04.140
and their deployments.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:05.520
TODD SIEGEL: Thanks, Cynthia.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:09.940
So there's two primary ways that
Twitter uses the Google edge.

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:14.040
So we use Google's POP network,
which is all over the world.

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:16.840
And we accept two
types of requests

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:18.240
through the load balancer.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:21.600
One is Layer 4 only, and
the other is Layer 7.

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:23.400
So in this first
example, we're looking

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:26.670
at how Twitter uses
Google's TCP proxy.

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:30.060
So in this case, the Google
edge closest to the user

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:34.140
that will steer the user to
based on our traffic control

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:38.190
tower system, will terminate
TCP for the end user,

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:40.410
establish a
persistent connection

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:44.070
with a Twitter service, which
is running in GCP, the Google

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:45.570
Compute Region.

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:48.810
And then we will then proxy
that TV connection back

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:52.890
to one of many Twitter VIPs.

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:54.780
In this diagram
here you see there's

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:59.250
a blue, a purple,
and an orange VIP

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:02.930
that the Twitter service
and GCP will hand off to.

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:04.930
These represent different
paths back to Twitter.

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:08.069
So some of them ride
on the Google backbone

00:17:08.069 --> 00:17:09.670
as far as possible.

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:12.780
Some of them hand off as
quickly as possible from Google

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:16.829
to the Twitter backbone,
or private interconnect.

00:17:16.829 --> 00:17:21.839
So in this case,
using Layer 4 only,

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:26.160
provide some additional
security for Twitter in cases

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:29.430
where we want the
certificate to always remain

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:33.570
in the Twitter POP, or in
Twitter infrastructure.

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:36.640
Or also provides us with
some additional compatibility

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:39.060
if we have some
Twitter clients that

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:43.680
have special Twitter
only Level 7 behavior,

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:49.350
and various protocol,
idiosyncrasies, et cetera.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:53.250
Additionally we use Google
pretty heavily as a Layer 7

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:56.980
proxy and we get multiple
benefits in this case.

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:58.470
So again, we set
up multiple VIPs

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:03.510
that all correspond to
the worldwide Google edge.

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:07.040
And the different
VIPs use the internet

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:10.530
NEG that Cynthia was
talking about before,

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:14.610
to route back to one
of n different Twitter

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:16.015
back in that piece.

00:18:16.015 --> 00:18:18.390
And these IPs, again, they
correspond to different routes

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:21.570
back to Twitter either over the
Google backbone or the Twitter

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:23.640
backbone.

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:26.193
And again, we measure
those and send the user

00:18:26.193 --> 00:18:28.110
to whichever one could
be performing the best,

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:31.570
or perhaps where we need
additional capacity.

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:33.765
And I'll talk
about that shortly.

00:18:33.765 --> 00:18:35.640
In this case, as you
can see, the Google edge

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:36.780
does everything.

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:40.140
There's no service that
Twitter has to run inside GCP,

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:41.800
there's no deployments
we have to do,

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:46.260
there's no monitoring we have to
do besides overall Google Load

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:47.880
balancer monitoring.

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:49.740
So this has been
really great for us

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:53.430
and reduced the amount of
operations and deployments

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:57.660
that we've had to do to GCP.

00:18:57.660 --> 00:19:01.410
Let's take a look at what this
has done for our end users.

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:03.420
With a combination
of the control tower

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:05.490
and expanding our
edge to regions

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:09.090
in two locations in
the public Cloud,

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:11.850
we've been able to pretty
dramatically improve

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:15.400
the performance for
many of our users.

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:17.580
This graph here shows
the improvements

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:22.320
at various percentiles
p25, p75, p99 for users

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:23.460
in different countries.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:28.620
And this represents the user
measured at home timeline

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:31.590
request latency end to end.

00:19:31.590 --> 00:19:34.170
So it includes all the back
end latency, the connection

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:38.270
establishment latency, transit
latency, et cetera, et cetera.

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:42.300
And as you can see in
all of these countries,

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:43.980
we've pretty
dramatically improved

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:45.810
some of the [INAUDIBLE]
latency, and even

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:50.470
some of the earlier
percentile latency as well.

00:19:50.470 --> 00:19:54.900
We've seen this correspond to
greater satisfaction and usage

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:56.580
of Twitter in these
regions as well,

00:19:56.580 --> 00:19:59.130
as failures have gone down.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.250
And requests success
rate has gone up

00:20:01.250 --> 00:20:03.510
and leniency has gone down.

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:07.030
One additional benefit from
using the public cloud edge

00:20:07.030 --> 00:20:09.840
is from using Google
load balancer has

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:13.860
been additional capacity
that we've got it to use

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:17.130
with no deployment on our end.

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:21.840
This chart comes from a
recent event in South America.

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:25.230
There's a reality TV show
called Big Brother Brazil that

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:28.380
recently set the Guinness
Book of World Records

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:30.040
most online votes.

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:33.520
It took this away from
American idol, I believe.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:35.490
And this purple line
here shows the number

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:40.080
of requests that happened
at the peak of voting

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:43.290
during this show, the number
of requests to Twitter.

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:44.790
And the red line
on the bottom shows

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:47.025
how much Twitter
on-prem capacity

00:20:47.025 --> 00:20:49.468
that we have in South America.

00:20:49.468 --> 00:20:51.510
And this peak was actually
three times the amount

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:54.070
of on-prem capacity
that we have.

00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:57.760
So if we weren't able to
burst into the public cloud

00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:00.450
and spill over into
our other regions,

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:02.460
Twitter likely would
have been just hard

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:05.930
down for the entire
continent of South America.

00:21:05.930 --> 00:21:09.210
And by using the control
tower to steer users

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:13.670
to public cloud
endpoints and spillover

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:16.410
events and other things
that are performing well,

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:20.040
we not only kept
Twitter successfully up.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:21.600
But we were able
to keep Twitter up

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:24.240
with really fantastic
performance for our end users

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:25.930
at this time.

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:29.700
Let me hand it off to Cynthia
to talk a bit about the Traffic

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:31.178
Director and hybrid network.

00:21:31.178 --> 00:21:32.220
CYNTHIA THOMAS: So, yeah.

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:34.020
Let's talk a little
bit Traffic Director.

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:36.590
For those who don't
know, Traffic Director

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:39.900
is a managed control plane
enabling global services

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.030
with various types of
back ends and GCPs.

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:46.290
So that could include
GCE instances or GKE,

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:49.860
and programmatically
configures envoy proxies.

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:51.870
So it actually leverages
the Google Cloud load

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:53.605
balancer and this
is a powerful way

00:21:53.605 --> 00:21:58.240
to serve applications globally,
and we have a global network.

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:02.410
Traffic Director additionally
allows for hybrid connectivity,

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:06.040
as it has the capability
to route traffic

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:08.540
to non GCP back ends.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:11.080
So it's an additional way
Twitter can direct traffic

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:13.000
to its data centers.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:14.770
All right, thanks for
watching our talk.

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:15.687
We made it to the end.

00:22:15.687 --> 00:22:18.520
We hope you learned a little
bit about how Twitter scales

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.900
with Google edge networking.

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:24.610
Wanted to also-- if you wanted
more information about Traffic

00:22:24.610 --> 00:22:26.380
Director, we have
follow up talks

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:29.650
that will dive deeper into the
Traffic Director abilities,

00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:32.640
amongst many of the
other networking talks.

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:35.710
I want to also have a
special thanks to Todd

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:37.540
and the Twitter
team for walking us

00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:40.120
through how Twitter
edge deployment is

00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:42.858
using Google Cloud.

00:22:42.858 --> 00:22:43.650
TODD SIEGEL: Great.

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:44.650
Thanks so much, Cynthia.

00:22:44.650 --> 00:22:46.800
And a big thank you to
Cynthia and to the Google

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:49.500
team on this great partnership.

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:51.990
We have more resources about
